    id: "287",
    text: "eel\n\nao WEES Se sows 5 Vs eae TS ee\n\nTHE MANLY ART.\n\nHEENAN AND MACE AT THE VARIETIES\n\n:\n\nOutpeuring of the Fancy.\n\n’ ”\n\nAn “Assault at Arms’’ Between the\nPugilists.\n\nA Delighted Audience.\n\nFew cities of the Union can boast of a larger number\nof admirers of the manly art of self-defence than St.\nLou. Not only haye we hundreds of amateur boxers,\nbut a fair proportion of professional pugilists, and\nthose of no mean pretensions. The herculean Mike\nMcCoole, who was at one time regarded as the best man\nin America, is a prominent resident in thecity. Tom\nAllen, who now wears the colors of champion, is alsoa\ndenizem here. Charley Gallagher, who won a match\nwith Alien, kept a saloon here for several months. Bill\nDavis sometimes makes this his home. Jim Coyne\nliyed here for a short time, and Wm. Ryall died here.\nBesides these heavy weights, we havea number of\nlight weights of acknowledged skill, and several retired\n\nWith such an array of buffers in our midst, it is not\nstrange that a growing fondness for the bloody en-\ncouniers of the roped arenashould be observable among\nour people. This partiality for the prize ring is not\nconfined to the eamsgcet | classes and saloon-keepers, but\nextends to the more refined walks of life; and the oc—\ncurrence of a prize fight between tried pugilists, within\na reasonable distance of the city is sure to cail to the\na! a goodly crowd of gentlemen of respectable social\n\nOD.\n\npeThe announcement, therefore, that those famous\nwee John C, Heenan and James...",
