    localCopyEdited: "images/articles/image-300.jpg",
    localCopyFull: "images/articles/image-300b.jpg",
    id: "300",
    text: "THE COMING PRIZE FIGUT,\nAttempt to Assassinate Bill Davis,\n\nThe national “mill” for the pugilistic champion-\nship between Bill Davis, of St. Louis, and Tom\nAllen, of California, to come off on the 12th of Jan-\nuary, is excitinga vast amount of attention in that\neurious world which revolves around the prize ring.\nAs the day approaches the feeling deepens intoa\npassionate rivairy between the adherents of the con-\ntestants, until om one side It would seem they\nwould even dare the guilt of murder to\nsave the money which bas been staked on the re-\nsalt, as whoever fails to appear on the ground will\nlose the match. No wonder good people raise their\nhands in holy horror at prize-fighting and exclaim,\n“Can such things be” in a Christian country. It is\nvery sad indeed, but such things do exist, and will\nbe to the end of the chapter, so far as present ap-\npearances go. The tollowing particulars afford an\nillustration:\n\nOn Christmas day some friends of Allen an’ sev-\neral friends of Bill Davis went out to the Fair\nGrounds to see Bill in training under the manipu-\nlation of “Dablin Tricks.” Efforts are reported to\nhave been made to bribe “Dublin Tricks” to break\ndown Davis, but, as usual, Bill Blake is unbribable,\nBut as nothing could be done in that direction, an-\nother dodge was tried more desperate in character.\nSeven or eight rowdies, said to be backers of Allen,\ndetermined to render Davis powerless. So, on\nChristmas night, at Doyle's, near the Fair Grounds,\nDavis’ headquarters in...",
