    text: "AFTER THE BATTLE.\nA Chat With Tom Allen-McCoole’s Condition Sc.\n\n‘When the Uncle of the Nephew drove into Paris\nafter the campaign which terminated in a blaze of\nglory st Marengo, the streeta, t'e¢ windows an! the\nhouse-tops of that gay capital swarmed with the\npopulace,eager to gaze upon and do honor to the con-\nquveror. Bérthier’s weak head was fairly upset by\nthe music, the ilfuminstions, and the shouts of the\nexcited people, and, turning to his master, he con-\ngratulated him upon his vast amd enduring fame.\n“Bah!” said the practical Napoleon—“‘what is fame\nbut a great noise? There would be just as large a\n\n_ crowd to see me guillotined as there is now to we!-\ncome me back to Paris!\"\n\nThe probabitity is that Napoleon was exactly and\nprecisely right in his estimate of the qaestionable ar-\nticle which men seek at the cannon’s mouth and\nether less dangerous localities. So, at least, we thought\nTuesday evening, while Fifth street was blockaded\nby a throng striving to catch 2 glimpse of burly Mike\nMeCoole, as that battered hero descemfed trom his\ncarriage, amd sought balm for his wounds and\nwoes im the retirement of private life. Fran‘ic\nbuzzas tore the air,and caps were dung skyward\nwhen the champion showed himself at the window,\nand then the inconstant mob streamed off toward\nTom Allen's saloon to do the same things there. For\nhours the sidewalks were jammed and the cars over-\nflowing with pilgrims vibrating between these two |\npugilistic shrines, and yesterday the...",
