    text: "PISTIC INTELLIGENCE.\nA Timid Reporter Visits the Champion.\n\nOne of our reporters, who is not & very muscular\nperson, yesterday paid a visit to Mr. Michael Mc-\nCoole, at his quarters on Fifth street. The reporter\nentered cautiously, with but little hope of finding\nMcCoole in his bar-room, in the belief that be was\nnot in condition to put in 8 public appearance; but,\nto his great satisfaction, he descrie] Mac standing at\nhis counter, talking with two gentlemen, to whom\n(without intending it) he heard the champion re-\nmark, “Well, tell him that’s what I say—I'll stand\nby that.” This was supposed to refer to his chal-\nlenge to Allen. It would be difficult to describe the\nappearance of the champion ashe stood. The fact\n{s our reporter is afraid to do it if he could.\nHe is too timid. It wont do to tell the truth\nat all times. The two gentlemen with whom\nMRP Siw sowed a little to one side,\nleaving a space ‘vacant im front oi Ma, wus,\nwith considerable trepidation, our reporter made a\nforward movement. “Mr. McCoole,” (there was a\ndryness in the reporter’s throat, for which he was\nunprepared), “I am reporter for the RE—Re—pvsxi-\naN, and ——” (looking down in some confusion at\nthe recollection of his report of the late fight),\n“Weil!” says Mac—“and would like to have « few\nminutes conversation with you.” It is said that\nwhen a mzn can see only with one eye,the optical pow-\ners of two are concentrated into the one, the nerves\nof the blind one lending the other additional ex-\npreasion....",
