    text: "A PUGILISTIC LION.\nrn ———_-<--—---——\nRETURN OF TOM ALLEN.\na -- or\nHow He Was Received at Louisville.\nee\n\nThe champion pugilist of America, Tom Allen,\nreturned bome yesterday from Cincinna‘l, where he\nspent a few days with his old Epglish sweetheart,\nTom was immensely lionized at Porkopolis, and the\nvillages through -which he pas:ed. He 13 jabilant,\nand is willing to fight anv manu in America for a\npuree of $1000 to $5000. As to Charley Gallagher.\nwho laid him out last winter by a lucky blow in the\nsecond round, he fs so eager to get at the tall Cana-\ndian that he 1s willing to put up $2500 agains;\nCharley’s $1500. Tom wants to fight within fifty\nmiles of Cincinnati. James Coyne, a light weight,\ncame with Allen. He also is ‘‘spoiling forafight.’’\n\nThe Louisville Courier, of the 6th, gives the fol-\nlowing account of Allen’s recep 10n in that city:\n\nTom Allen, the hero of the late prize fight, arriv-\neo in town yesterday afternoon by the train from\nCircirnati. He was detained several hours by the\naccident this side of Covington. While tae trestie\nwork of the fallen bridge was nee repaired, with\nMr. Jobn Shaw, of this city, and four or five cth-\ners, he took a hard car and started dowa the road\nin the direction ot Lomsvitle. The party soon got\ntired, however, and even Tcm’s great muscle sott-\nened in‘propelling the unwieldy Machine up some\nof the long ard steep grades in that vicini'y; so they\nhalted at the firs: station’ and awaited the coming\nup of the delayed train. In the...",
