    text: "= <Eé.\n(ae a °\n‘FIGHT.\nSS\n\nELEVEN ROUNDS IN TWENTY-\nSIX MINUTES. |\nSeti 5 Sacele |\n\n|\n\nAllen the Winner, but the Fight\nDecided a Draw. a\n\nFirst Blood and First Knock Down\nfor Gallagher.\naang pinot est ins\n\nSeenes on the Beat and at\n. the Bing.\n\n—— ——\n\nA“ DESCRIPTION OF THE MEN AND\nTHEIR BATTLES.\nns\n\nThe much talked of prize fight between Tom Allen, —\nthe famous English pugilist, and Charley Gallagher, the\nIrish Canadian, took place, as announced, at Foster\nIsland, about 30 miles below this city.\n\nThe steamboat Louisville, with s large crowd of rags-\nmuffins, shoulder-hitters, loafers, lunch-grabbers, pea-\nnut-eaters, short-haired, bull-necked bruisers, and a fair\nsprinkling of respectable citizens of pugilistic propensi- |\n\nties, left the foot of Elm street about ten o’clock in the\nmorning for the seene of the conflict, |\n\nNo impediment was offered by tho authorities to tho\ndeparture of the motley crowd of excursionists, and\nhundreds of people gathered on the bank of the river to\nsee them embark,\n\nAs public taste demands full accounts of these brutal\nprize fighte, a deputation from the reportorial corps of\nthe Democrat was sent on the voyage, with instructions\n\nto send back early and faithful reports, |\n\nSome account of the combatants may not be out of\nplace, and we commence with a sketch of\n\nTOM ALLEN, |\n\nAllen came over from England in company with Bill\nGoes, champion of England, to a draw in long battle:,\nand their reputation naturally preceeded them, more\n\nparticularty...",
