    text: "if\n\n: THE PRIZE FIGHT.\n\ns ————\n\nn| Allen and Gallagher in the Ring.\n\n| eh\n\n_ The Combatants at Foster's Island.\n\ne\n\n» | ——\n\n>| A HARD FOUGHT BATTLE.\n\n: | THE SPONGE THROWN UP.\n\ne ALLEN CLAIMS THE ViCTORY. |\n\na |\n\na | iota ia\n\n: | The Referee Decides the Fight a Draw.\n\nn\n\nt Our columns latterly have cemtained such a sur-\n\n‘ feit of pugilistic literature that we shall spare the\n\n\" | reader any further infliction of the «xme nature, ex-\n\ns | ceptso far as may be necessary for a correct unaer-\n\n- | standing of the circumstances attendant upon the\n\ny present battle.\n\n“ First, then, as fo the men themselves.\n\nby TOM ALLEN.\n\nThis worthy member of the puffing brotinerhood\n\n|. | was born at Birmingham, England, April 23d, 1519,\n\n1 | and consequently twenty-nine summers have flown\n\n_ | over his innocent head. As soon as young Thomas\n\ni) | was able to “stand on his pins” he began to handlé\nhis “‘mauleys,” and pounded his playmates unti>\n\n' there was not a whole pose or an unblacked eye\n\nn } among the urchins in the parish to which said\n\n€ | Thomas belonged. As he grew tomanhood his mus-\n\ni cle and fists enlarged, and his combativeness in-\n\nn | creased in the same proportiest, so that at twenty-\n\nF one he was ready to put his foot onthe lower round of\n\n_ | fame’s ladder and begin to climb toward that gallant\n\n- | tree where grow the fistic laurels. Accordingly, in\n\n+ | 1861 he met a gentleman named Mortis Connor and\n\n~ | fought him for $25 aside. Connor was demolished\nin twelve rounds,...",
