    text: "ee\nCard from Jem Yince.\n\nA card last night appeared in the Dispatch, which had\nsot Tom Allen's signature, but was apparentiy from\nlim, stating that Mace desired to make the coming bat-\ntle a national affair between himself as champion of\nEngland, and Allen, (now acitizen,) who is champion\nof the United States. In order to deny any such desice\nor intention, Mace requests that the following card be\nputlished:\n\n_V4kIETIES TREATER, Sr. Louis, March 23, 1876.\nMitters Missouri Dermecrst:\n\nI observe with regret, in the Dispatch of last evening,\nan item evidently msertes at the request of Mr. Tom\nAller, as an advertisement of hie individual pretensions,\nin which he endeavors to convey an erroneous ides of hie\n\ntrue position with respect to myself and our forthcom-\nipg contest. He complains that I have not called upon\nhim. Incommon courtesy, he should have remem-\nbered that Iam a stranger here, and he should have\nfirst visited me. Had he done so, I would have re—\nceived him and returned his visit with pleasure,\n\nConcerning his attempt to cultivate popular sympa-\nthy by a sham arigga, coincgs 4 of the United States in our”\nproposed meeting, I have enone: f to way that he is av\nmuch an Englishman aa myself, has been here little\nlonger than I, and certainly has no more friendly feol-\ning for the land of his professed adoption then T hava\nThere can be no pretext whatever for giving to our fight\nany international character. I, at least, can see none,\nand only propose to whip Mr. Tom Allen...",
