    text: "|\nGATHERING OF THE FANCY.\n|\n\n‘ —————— LT\n\nFights at Wash Home and Trouble at\nthe Mansion House.\nA DISGRACEFUL AFFAIR\n\nThe fancy held high carnival yesterday at the Wash\nHome, a sporting rendezvous about four miles west of\nthe Court House, and near the St, Charles rock road.\nTom Allen is now training ‘at the Home for his\ncoming fight with Mace, and his presence,\nwith the addition of sparring, foot races,\nand other sperts announced to take place, were attrac-\ntions sufficient to draw a large crowd of gamblers,\nshoulder-hitters, sports and fast men tothe locality.\nThe large quantities of poor whisky consumed by the\ncrowd soon began to assert itself, and numerous fights\ntook place, in one of which | George Bowman,\nthe man who wrote the letter to Spahr which caused the\nslaying of Baldwin, in the Southern Stables, @ short\ntime since, received a severe cut in the back of the\nbead. The original cause of this fight, it was said, was\nthe fart of Dick Roche, (a well-known sporting\nman) reproaching Bowman with having been the cause\nof Baldwin's death. A free fight, at any rate, took\nplace, in which Bowman received a cut in the head and\nRoche was stabbea witha knife, but was not danger-\nonsly hurt. Bowman succeeded in getting away from\nthe crowd, and, entering his carriage, drove to the\nMansion House, where he was followed by Roche\nand quite a number of others. The trouble here re—\ncommenced and Bowman was obliged to jump from a\nwindow and fiee for his life. When doing so he was\nfired at...",
