    text: "| THE PRIZE FIGHT.\n\n- Allen Whips Gallagher Easily.\nSixteen Eounds Fonght—A Quiet But\n\n_ Bileedy Aair—Police Of the Track.\n\nThe fight between the pugilists Tom. Allen and J.\nC. Gallagher, came off early yesterday morning on a\nsmall island near the arsenal. After the recent fias29\nsteps were taken by the friends of the parties to ar-\nrange a contest which might take place without dan-\nger of interference, and it was agreed ostensibly to\nfight within fifty miles of Kansas city, on Tue«iay\nnext, and this false report was industrieusly clreula-\nted, and found its way into print. On Friday even-\ning it was determined that the fight should come off\non the following morning, and secret pre-\nparations were made for it. Reports\ncontrary to this were industriowsly circulated, an),\nby dint of bard lying, were believed by some, and the\npolice were kept ignorant of the proposed action.\nOnly a small number of persons were infirmed of the\naffair, and the profoundest secresy was enjoined upon\nthem. As aconsequence, instead of the fight being\nwitnessed by 500 or 604 persons, it was only witnessed\nby 40 or 50, and the scandal was in this way some-\nwhat diminished. Then, too, there was no prize-\nfight excursion with its demoralizfng scenes, and the\nbuffers lost their $1,000 or $1500.\n\nThe men fought only for the stakes—$1,500;\n$1,000 of which was placel by Allen against\nGallagher's $500. At half-past three yesterday morn-\ning, Looney and several others drove down to the\nboat Bertha, at the...",
