    text: "JOHNNY FRANKLIN,\nThe Adventurous Carecr of a Sporting\nMan Well Mrownin St. Louis.\nae\n(Cincinnati Commercial, 16th. }\n\nThe subject of this sketch was very well\nknown in this city from 1862 until three or four\n\nears @ince, among all classes of people who\noes anything about sporting matters. He was\na professional gambler, and had been for many\nYears; but his attention was not always en-\ngrossed merely by the gaming-house or houses\nin which ‘he happened to be inter-\nested. He was a ‘‘sporting man” in\nevery sense of the expression—on the race\ntrack, in the Spares oe and atthe prizering. He\nwas aman of striking appearance, of nedium\nheight, erect, compact 1®ime, straight and\nsquare. His hair and whiskers were tinged with\ngray. His features were reguiar, and his face\nwas one that would attract a second glance from\nthemost careless observer. His eyes, in par-\nticular, challenged attention—small, flashing,\nhazel-gray ae that penctrated whatever they\nwere turned upon, and would light up suddenly\ninto a tierce blaze when an angry thought passed\nthrough the brain back of thei.\n\nTo this man Cincinnati, Si. Louis and New\nOrleans were indebted—however questionable\nthe character of the business—for the occurrence\nofinany prize fights and attempts at ‘‘mills.”\nIt was Franklin who started the Jones-McCoole\nmatch, and the MeCoole-Coburn affair at this\npoint. He was responsible tor several fights\nnear St. Lonis, one of which resulted in the\nmagnificent drubbing of the giant MecCoole at\nthe...",
