    text: "ee: > ee ert OH\n| “THE RIVALS.”\nfeelers | =!\nAllen After Hogan with a Hot Stick—\n\nJack Mad“en wants a Shy at the Latter\n\n—Arthur Chambers at Liberty.\n\nBen Hogan istotake a benefit at the Theater\nCemiqne on Tuesday night, when he will spar with.\nJohn Sweeney, his late tramer. Sweeney is thé\nsame man who donned the mittens | with Hogan in\nKansas City. Tom Allen, hearmgiof Ben's con:\ntemplated yisit, publishes 4 card this\nmcrning, in order that it may catch Hogan's eye,\nbefore leaving the city. It will be nm that Allen\ngives odds of five to one, and asks only one pro-\nyiso, namely: that the fight shall take place\non th> quiet. Tom’s idea is to ve two news-\npaper men present, so that if any foul play should\nbe attempted it might be made public. Jack Mad-\nden, who last night expressed a pertect willingness\nto fight Hogan, kas some little reputation asa\nfighter. He and Hogan are about of a weight, and\nshould they come together, the baltile wili be an\nInteresting one.\n\nThis 1s what the champion has to ¥ on the sub\n\nact:\n? ST. Louis, Noyember 28, 1873. ~\nTo the Editor of the Democrat: |\n\nStr—I cee by a report. in that prejudiced paper,\nthe Globe, that Ben. Hogan is styl **Champion\nof America.” No man tan be champion of Amer-\nica without fighting for and — title, and\nmost assuredly the title cannot be clarmed by a\nman who gives all his portion of the stake money\nto a gang.of murderers, who not only broke upthe\nfight, but/threatened to kill me and my friends. If\n\nthere is any' fight...",
