    text: "—\n\nIt seems that, despite Tom's oft-arowed re-\ntirement from the ring, and in the face of his\nrecent denial of the report that he was about\nto make a match with George Rooke, he has\ngone East to arrange the preliminaries for that\nencounter, anid that a deposit of $500 a side,\nbeing one-sixth of the agreed stake, is now\nin the hands of Harry Hill.\nThe fight is arrangefl to take place withiu\nfifty miles of Pittsburg, on the 17th of June.\nThe chanees are about five to two that the\nfight will not occur, but as it will probably\nattract a good deal of attention from the sport-\ning world a brief review of the careers of the\ncombatants and of their respective pugilistic\nabilities will not be uninteresting.\n\nTom Allen is too well known to require any\ndetailed description and the sporting public\nis already familiar with his history. He is in\nprime health now, fat asa bear and will have\nto do some of the hardest training of his life to\nget down from his present 210 pound\nof adipose tothe 172 pounds of bone and\nmuscle requisite to fit him for the roped arena.\nMoreover Tom is getting\n\nA TRIFLE STALB. |\nNot that his age—36—has begun to tell on)\nhim, but he has trained down and then fat-\ntened up again so many times that his sys-—\ntem docs uot take to the flesh-reducing\nprocess as kindly as itused to. indeed it is\nseriously questioned by sporting men whether\nTom is not a better man at 200 0r 210 than at\n172. It is trne that, in his present flesh he\nmight be a trifle slow, and his wind...",
