    id: "521",
    text: "ALLEN AND ROOKE.\nAll About the Big Fight that\nDid Not Take Place.\n\nRooke Saves His Money by Plead-\n\n. ing the Gambling Act.\nAn Insight Into the Peculiar Workings of the\nRough Elemeni of America.\n\nIt is only necessary to announce_that the much-\ntalked-of pugilistic encounter between Allen and\nRooke has proved a fizzleto convince law-abiding\ncitizens that what was ence recognized a3 the\nmanly art by the nobility and aristocracy of the\nOld World is a defunct institutior, In olden times,\nwhen such clever gladiators as Tom Cribb, Tom\n\nSpring, Sayers, Hyer and Heenan fought for glo-\n\nry, and made the money involved a secondary\n\n| consideration, the sport was countenanced by the\nlaw, and all attempts at interference were cried\n\n“down by men in high places. In America, since\n\nthe day that Morrissey’s mob swindled Heenan out\n\nof his fight with the New York Congressman, a\nfistic encounter decided fairly and squarely on the\nmerits of the men has been a rarity. ,Pugil-\nists made matches for the purpose of\nrobbing their opponents, and the roughest\ngang at the ring side invariably gained\nthe day. Each section of the country has\nits representative mob of roughs, and each mob a\nrepresentative leader. As an instance of how\n‘¢mills,’? in late years, have been conducted,\nreference to the Coburn-Mace meeting at New Or-\nleans may be made. Coburn, who is recognized\nas a ‘‘duffer,’’ and Mace, the most skillful man\nthat ever put up his hands, faced each other for\n\nfour hours and not a blow was...",
