    text: ". WHAT PRIZE-FIGHT.\nJem Coyne, Donkey il gent d and Tom\nAllen All After Rooke.\n\nTom Allen yesterday received the following tele-\ngram from Arthur Chambers, who is filling a\nsparring exhibition with Billy Edwards at Syra-\ncuse, N. Y.:\n\nSYRACUSE, N. Y., June 2%, 1875.\nTom Allen, 417 Washington avenue:\n\nHave sent articles to Harry Hill’s for Rooke to\nsign to fight within fifty miles of Detroit for one\nthousand dollars a side.\n\n(Signed) ARTHUR CHAMBERS.\n\nThis was in response.to the telegram sent to\nPhiladelphia by Allen, a few days ago, which was\nforwarded to Chambers.\n\nThe champion also received a letter yesterday\nfrom Jem Coyne, of Newark, who fought a draw\nwith Rooke, whenout of condition, a couple of\nyearsago. Jem states that Rooke, as usual, is\ndoing his fighting through the newspapers. He\necntemplates making another match with his old.\nantagonist, and wants Tom to second him. Teddy,\naliag ‘*Donkey’’ Neary, a brother to Fiddler\nNeary, is also anxions to have ago with Rooke,\nand if nothing comes from Allen’s last cartel, will,\ndoubtiess, be matched against him. He and Coyne\nare lighter men than Rooke, but are more en-\ntific. A may ortwo willshow whether Rooke in-\ntends entering the equared circle or not.",
