    id: "524",
    text: "- SCHOOL BOARD.\nThe Sale of the Washington School\nProperty Ordered.\n\nA Spat Between Messrs. Stanton and\nPeacock—Personal Explanations by\nMessrs. Meyer and Rassieur—Mr.\nLippman Reappointed to Committee\non Supplies, Etc.\n\nA called meeting of the Board of Public Schools\nwas held last night, President Richeson in the\nchair, **for the purpose of considering and dispos-\ning of the unfinished business of the last meeting\nand such other matters as might be brought be-\nfore the board.’’? The meeting was called at the\nrequest of Mr. J. Lippman and three other mem-\nbers of the board.\n\nWASHINGTON SCHOOL.\n\nMr. Brolaski stated that the unfinished business\nof the board was the sale of the property on which\nthe Washington School was located, and moved\nthat the property be sold to the highest bidder\nunder the direction of the Committees on Ways and\nMeans and on Claims.\n\nMr. Peacock said he had received very extraor-\ndinary terms in the way of rooting property,\naud he od hg that, unless a certain figure was\nreached, the property ought not to be leased. He\nmoved that the sale, if made, be under the direc-\ntion, also, of the Leasing Committee.\n\nMr. Stanton was strongly opposed to the idea of\nleasing the Property « He was informed that the\nparties who wished to lease it were not the best\ncharacters in the world. ‘The School Board ought\nto be ashamed to lease the school building as a\ndance-hall or a bawdy-house. If pugilists and\nerste introduce themselves to the Leasing\nJommittee and ask to lease...",
