    text: "O’ Baldwin is dead. He died as most of the clan\nlo, from the effects of a wound; for the professors\nit the manly art of self- defense are the most prone\no cowardly assassination, thrusts with glittering\nteel, and sudden jeopardy from powder and lead.\nTe was shot by his partner in the administration\nsf a ‘*gin-mill,’’ the quarrel originating in a dis-\nmute over the dissolution of their coparsnership.\nHe departed this life in New York, at lla. m.\nesterday, and alife that was of little use to the\nworld is forfeited, and but little regret felt for its\noss. Aman of great strength, considerable\nluck and fair intelligence leaves on the records of\nime only the remembrance that he was a bully, a\nwrniser, & prize-fighter—a foreigner who spent\n1alf of his eight years in this country as an occu-\nsant cf her prisons. :\n\nO’ Baldwin has . j\n\nAST, LOUIS HISTORY,\nyrief as itis, having been vanquished by a local\nfladiator ina glove contest, and ‘‘at the time of\n1is demise’’ there were negotiations on foot to\nmake a match between him and Tom Allen, the\nocal celebrity spoken of.\n\nA reporter of the GLOBE DEMOCRAT called on\nrom yesterday afternoon, at his saloon, on Wash-\nngton avenue, and, the customary civilities hav-\nng been exchanged, gained from the redoubtable\nind invincible pugilist some points of the defunct\n\nugilist’s hlography. O'Baldwin was born in\nWaterford, Ireland, in 1839, oes his years\nill 1861 in that historic town, ma 4 to the re-\nspectable height of six feet six inches, and...",
