    text: "2 ee he ee ee ;\nThe Gladiators of the Arena to\nCome Together,\n\nMonsieur Theobaud Bauer, the champion\nwrestler, is still in the city, but nothing definite\nhas yet been heard from Christol, who expressed a\nwillingness to have a bout with any man whom\nTom Allen could produce. Allen left for Louis-—\nville yesterday, and last night gave a sparring ex-\nhibition in that city, assisted by his trainers, young\nDonnelly and Jack Madden, as well as local box-\ners. He will be back this morning, when the\nfollowing communication from Christol, received\n\nat this office yesterday, will doubtless receive his\n| attention:\nTo the Editor of the Globe-Democrat:\n\nCnicaGo, March 10, 1875.—Dear Sir: I have\nseen in your honorable journal the conversation\nwith Mr. Tom Allen, in which he desires to back\nBauer to wrestle against me. If M. Bauer is will-\ning to come to Chicago, lam ready to meet him.\nI will be in Chicago for six weeks, and itis beter\nfor me to wrentie bere than to go to St. Louis.\n\nYours respectfully. ANDRE CHRISTOL,\n\nChampion of France.\n\nIn view of the fact that Allen brought Baner out\n\nhere from Boston, on the assertion of a Well-\n\n_ known Chicago friend that Christol would wrestle\n\n| him here, the above epistle looks very much as\nthough Andre wanted to back out. He doubtless\nappreciates the fact that Bauer is neither a Car-\nteron nora Rigal, but the most scientific wrestler\nin the world, as his record proves. Bauer has\nnever suffered defeat in this country, but has in\nturn defeated...",
