    id: "541",
    text: "FRUIT FOR SPORTS.\nJoe Goss, the Famous Pugilist, Arrives\n| ae in America.\n\nHis Intentions--Plain Talk from Allen to Coburn--\nSchroeder’s Bluff at Bauer Not to be Noticed\n--The Ball Field,\n\nEncounters in the squared circle, owing princi-\npally to vigorous legislation against the prize\nring, have been of rare occurrence during the\npast few years, but from present indications this\nstate of affairs will not last long. Yesterflay\nmorning Tom Allen received a dispatch from Jem\nCoyne, of Newark, N. J., announcing thé arriyal\nin this country of Joe Goss, the famous pug of\nWolverhampton, Englagd. Jem Mace was to\nhave accompanied Joe to America, but as the\ntelegram does not méntion his name, it ié safe to\npresume that the clever gypsy abandoned the\nidea of revisiting the land of the stars and stripes\nfor the time being, at least. On March 5, 1967,\n\n/ ALLEN AND GOSS |\nmetina te ones Ba ger Baty ring for £100 a side and\nthe championship of middle weights. After fight-\ning thirty-four rounds, occupying one hour and\nfifty-two minutes, the battle was declared adraw.\nWhether Goss intends to challenge Allen for the\nAmerican .championship is not known. His\n\n‘chances to win are not looked upon as\nfavorable. Goss has had no. experience\nsince the fight in ‘67, while Allen has par-\nticipated in no less than nine encounters\nsince that time, and has improyec wonderfully\nsubsequently to his departure from Merrie En-\ngland. It is more than probable that if Mace is not\nwith Goss, Joe will be in St....",
