    text: "COBURN TO BAUER. .\n\nThe Pugilist Anxious to Mect the Wrest-\nler—Joe’s Proposition—A Letter from\nMace. ait\nThe arrival of Joe Coburn and the departure o!\n\n| Tom Allen continue to be are. interest to that\nportion of the community which delights in seeing\nfistic’ encounters and talking slang. Joo hag\nchanged very little in appearance since his visit\nhere in 1868, when old Bill Ryall and Tom Allen\nfirst came to this ‘‘blarsted’’ country, and the\nmanner in which he put up his hands at the New\n\nAdelphi Theater last ni@fit denoted that neither of\n\nthem had lost its cunning. Ned Donnelly, Allen’s\n\nopponent in many mimic encounters, was the\nparty of the other part, and the set-to wasa\ncharmer.\n\nIn the ticket office of the theater the nian who\nwent over to Ireland to fight Mace was“aterview-\nedin relation to his assertion that he had come\nWest to find Bauef and wrestle him. Coburn\npersisted that such was the case, and produced a\ncheck for $250 which he was anxious to deposit as\naforfcitin some good man’s hands to bind the\nmatch. He states that while Bauer was in Boston\nhe endenvored to make a match on terms which the\nFrenchman intimated would be acceptable. Co-\nburn’s proposition at that time, and the one\nwhich he now advances, was that he and his op-\nponent should wrestle Greco-Roman style, with\nthe exception that each man was to be allowed to\nuse his legs in any manner he saw fit. This Bauer\nconceded, Coburn acknowledging that in the\nlegitimate Greco-Roman, stylez he was no...",
