    text: "Ese i 4\nBy Telegraph. ‘\n\nINDIANAPOLIS, May 3.—Chicago, 17; Capital\nCity, 10.\n\nBhe Prize Ring.\n\nAs an advertiser, Joe Goss, the pugilist, stands\nunrivaled. After having it heralded that he had\ndeposited $500 with Eph Holland, to make a match\nwith Allen, he ‘‘gives bimself dead away’’ a few\ndays later by publishing the subjoined challenge\nin the New York papers:\n\nCOLOSSEUM GROUNDS, {\n: New YoRK, May 2, 1876.\nTo the Editor of the Herald:\n\nAs there seems to be very general misapprehen-\nsion in sporting circles concerning the purpose of\nmy visitto America, andas there have appeared\nnumerous and unauthorized paragraphs in various\nponer: journals throughout the United States,\n\nwould like to state that my object in coming over\nhere is to arrange, if possible, a contest for the\nchampionship anda rer stake ; and I herewith\nchallenge Mr. Tom Allefi, now of St. Lonis, to\nfight nea eyaare stand-up fight, aerate to the\nrules of the London ghar ring, for the champion-\nship of the world and $1,000 » side, or for as much\nmore a side as may be eens! agreed upon, the\ntight to take place four months from the date of\nsigning articles. The reason I can not enter the\nring souner than the four months, is that for that\nlength of time I am under engagement to travel\nalong with Mr. Mace with Howes & Cushing's\ncircus. l mean business, and am ready to put up\nat any time the necessary forfeit. Respectfully,\n\nJoserH Goss, Champion of England.\n\nIf his depositin Cincinnati is really there, and\nif it has...",
