    text: "a)\nSPURTING NOTES.\n——s\nTHE ST. LOUIS BROWNS PRE-\nSENT THE CHICAGOES\nWITHA WHOLE NEST\nOF GOOSE EGGS—\nSCORE ITO O.\n\nAllen and Kelly «Last Night—A\nHandsome Tassle Between\nBauer and Christol—To-\nnight’s Match at\nthe Temple.\n\nYesterday afternoon the match gime be-\ntwen the White Stockings, of Chicago, and\nBrowns, ef St, Louis, was called at 4 o’clock\nsharp, at Grand Ayenue Park.in the pres-\nence of 2,000 spectators. The tirst inning in\nthe game gave the St. Louis nine the victory,\nClepp scoring one. From this until the\nclose of the ninth inning both clubs played\nwith rare skill. Neither side could score a\nrun, and base hits were as rare a3 errors.\nThe Whites labored with grim determins-\ntion, but the wily Browns, ever on the\nalert, sent them out with a goose egg every\ntime, while in eight of the nine innings the\nBrowns were retired with an0Q. The gam2\nlasted one hour and thirty minutes, an] was\nas rare an exhibition of skill at the bat orin\nthe field as ever witnessed. The victory\nwas somewhat of a surprise for St. Louis.\nSince the opening of the season the career of\nthe Whites has been a series ot victories.\nThey have defeated the erack clubs of Cin-\n‘innati and Louisville, which got away with\nthe Browns with apparent ease, and it was\nrensonable to suppose they would achieve a\nvictory yesterday, snd were conse tentiv\nthe favorites by odds, among the sporting\neeumuunity, who lost heavily upon the un-\nexpected result. This afternoon, the weather\npermitting, the second game of the...",
