    localCopyFull: "images/articles/image-557b.jpg",
    id: "557",
    text: "4 . t\nTOM AND JOE. :\nThe Articles Which Bind Allen and Goss\nto Fight.\nMecting of the Men for the First Time Since Their Merry\nMill in the Squared Circle. b\n\n{From the Cincinnati Commerrial of yesterday. J\n\nBy arrangement, Joe Goss and Tom Allen met\nyesterday afternoon, at the Empire Saloon, to\nagree on terms for their fght,and to sign the nec-\nessary articles that should make the same bind-\ning. At2p.m. the two big fighters were seated\nout in the summer-room in the rear of the saloon,\nunder the awning, and around them, in the\nroom, several groups of their friends and others,\nincluding representatives of the Gazette, Enquirer\nand Commercial, and quite a number of sporting\nmen. Back of them, in the pool-room, was adense\npack of less favored people, kept back by a guard, |\npeering in roms door and windows, and —\nmanifesting an absorbing interest in everything |\nthat was done and every word they could hear.\nAt Alien’s right hand was Ben De Bar, ready to.\nput up necéssary money, and at Goss’ elbow was\nJack Brown, représenting Jem Mace, one of\nGoss’ backers. Allen and Goss were talking |\nthin over in a business-like manner, quite —\ncoolly. A scribe having been secured to draw up |\nthe articles, and pens, ink and paper and a table\nfurnished, the work of making this strange con- |\ntract was qnite velfiberately entered on. It was |\nconsidered and executed as calmly as though the\ntwo men were engaging to go ona spurring tour, |\nand notto mect each other in a space twerty- |\nfour feet...",
