    text: "Jem Mace—The Object of His Visit to\nAmerica—His Opinion of Tom Allen\nand of Other Pugilists.\n\n{From the Buffalo News,}-\n\nIt being unlikely that Buffalo *will again be\nvisited by aman of so great a pugilistic fame as\nJem Mace, and as many have not had an oppor-\ntunity to see the celebrity,a Sunday News reporter\ncalled upon the gentlemap to obtain hia views as\nto men and events. He found him in company\nwith Mr. Taylor in the dressing-room of the Ter, |\n\n\\\nrace Theater, now under the efMficient manage-\nment of Williams, Knapp & Redmond, and was\nreceived with great cordiality and with tho ex-\npression: ‘‘I amentirely at your sérrice.** Both\nmenu were about donning their street clothes, and\nthe reporter had a good opportunity to note their\nsplendid physical manhood. Their frames are\nmassive and appear perfect in every part. There\nwas one thing particularly noticeable, which was\nthe entire absence of swaggerin their manner and\nspeech. There was, indeed, a conscious pride of\ntheir superiority, bal that, of course, is pardon-\nabic in men who have earned the right to so feel.\n\nWhen he bad gratified his curiosity tne reporter\n\ncommenced a conversation, as follows:\n\nReporter. I would like to obtain your views on\nthesubject of pugilism.\n\nMr. Mace. You are on the Sunday News, you\nsay?\n\nkh. Tam.\n\nMr. M. Imecd. TI understand that the Sunday\nNews is a very enterprising and upright paper.\n\nR. That is its aim. > hi\n\nMr. M. I shall answer with pleasure any ques-\ntion you may ask.\n\nR. How long...",
