    text: "Joe and Tom.\n{From the Pittsburg Leader. J\n\nLast evening a Leader reporter ran across Sam\nFrench, who used to keep sporting headquarters\non Tenth street, but who has been wasting his\ntime and means in McKeesport since last January.\nIt occurretl to the Leader man that Sam might\nknow something of Joe Goss, who is matched to\nfight Tom Allen on the 7th of September for a\n— of $5,000, the contest to take place withina\n\nundred miles of Cincinnati, probably in Ken-\ntucky. To the first question, as to whether he\nknew Joe, Sam replied:\n\n*‘Know him? Well, [think!I do. I took Joe\nGoss out of a booth, atraveling show, when he\nwas a lad of eighteen or twenty, when he was\nsparring for a living, and introduced him into\ncompany that made him worthy to stand up be-\nfore such a man as Jem Mace. I backed him in\nall hie Aghts. I saw him fight Allen nine years\nago; I think I may say I am pretty well psi Cape\n| with him. I went to school with his wife, so I\nought to say I am somewhat acquainted with both\nofthem.’’ 5\n\n_ Being satisfied that for information on the prom-\nised pugilistic event of the Centennial year in\nAmeriéa, the proper source had been siyuck, the\nreporter next inquired what Sam thonght of the\nprospects for a fight. He thought it would be a dead\nsquare battle; that both men mean business, and\nboth are good men. He believed that it would\ntake thirty-five rounds to settle it at least, barring\naccidents, and may be more.\n\nReporter. What do you think of the result, Mr.\nFrench?\n\nFrench....",
