    text: "OPINIONS.\n\nHow the Fight Takes onthe Street.\n\nThe fact is apparent that prize-tighting\nin this country is more popular than buli-\nfizhting in Madrid. Nothinr has bern\ntalked of on the street to-day but\nthe Goss-Allen fight. In the\nhotels, bar-rooms, among the rich and\npoor, the subject wasaliengrossing. The\nsentiment throughout the city is devidediy\nin favorof Allen. They consider that he\nbas been grossly wronged, and that the job\nwas set up weeks before to-(luy to de\nfeat Alien by fair means if possible,\nbut By foul means if necessary. Men on\nthe bet in this city will put up their purses\non Allen as long as acent remiins. and\nthey wiil back him eganst the known or\nunknown. ‘The feeciiag is that Aileo tas\nbeen beaten, throug. th: fvulest\nof means, but by Do means\nvanqui-bed. The championship of the\nworld is yet uvdecided, and itis by no\nmeans probable that Tom Allen will sur-\nrender the belt he has won by so much\nhonest, hard fighting.",
