    text: "MAY MORNING, SEP’\nFOUL FIGHTING.\nDetails of Yesterday’s Battle in\nthe Squared Circle,\nGraphic Picture of the Gathering\nat Cincinnati.\n\n' aT\nFriends of the Fistic Art Flock to the\n\nCity of Pork in Crowds,\nSeven Rounds Fought Without\nInterruption,\n\nAnd Then a Change of Venue is Ne-\ncessitated by the Appearance\nof the Militia.\n\nThe Scene of Battle Removed to\n\n. Another Neighboring Farm,\n\nAnd the Crowd Langh Defiance at Their\nBaffled Pursuers.\n\nOn the Twenty-First Round the Fight De-\n\ncided on a Claim of Foul.\n\nGoss Carried Away Beaten, While Allen\nWalks Off a Victim.\n\nThe Winner Loses by a Technical Interpretation of\n\nthe Rules.\n\nSpecial Dispatch to the Globe-Democrat.\n\nCINCINNATI, September 7.—After a deal of\ntrouble, the great Allen-Goss prize fight is at an\nend, and, as your correspondent has all along\nasserted in these dispatches, the St. Louis man\nhas been\n\nSHAMEFULLY ROBBED.\n\nOn Wednesday morning it was stated that Goss\nwould be all broken up in fifteen minutes; that\nAllen was manifestly the better man, but that he\ncould not win under any circumstances. All of\nwhich has turned out frue. Before referring to\nthe fight, and after appending the records of the\nmen, it may be well to give a litile inside history\nof the match.\n\nTHE ARTICLES.\n\nFirst of all, the following is a transcript of the\narticles of agreement, as signed by both men and\ntheir witnesses:\n\n**The undersigned, Thomas Allen and Joseph\nGoss, make this joint agreement at the City of\nCincinnati, in the presence of...",
