    text: "The Ring.\nDWYER AND ELLIOTT aT HOME.\nBpectal Dispatch to the Glebe- Democrat.\n\nNew YORK, May 11.—Jos. Eltiott, the puat-\nlist who was defeated by John Dwyer at Long\nPoint on Thursday last, arrived in Brooklyn\non Saturday, accompanied by his trainer Joe\nGoss. His face is bruised and discolored, but\nhis ribs were not broken, as has been stated.\nHe takes his defeat philosophically, and says\nthat he did his best. Age and impaired con-\nstitution and eye-sight, he claims, prevented\nhim from winning. In regard to the charge\nthat he made several fouls, he said, ‘‘the least\nmaid about that the better. Dwyer\nmade a foul in the frst of:\nthe = fight. On the second round\nhe@iruck me while Iwas down, and every- |\nbody about the ring saw it was a fonl.’’ El-\nlintt denies that he was left on the field by his.\nbackers, and says that Charlev Johnson, Robt.\nMooney, Goss and others did all in their power\nforhim. The reason that he left the battle-\nground in Dwyer’s boat was becanse his back-\ners had. no place on their tag where he could\nliedown. Hew still suffering severely from\nthe injuries to his rib#, and is now at\nRed Leary’s hotel, at Fort Hamiiton.\nCharley Johnson, Eiliott’s principal\nbacker, when asked what he thought\nof the fight, replica: “I - admit\nthat Dwyer is one of the hardest hitters ia tnis\ncountry, but I don’t think that his abilities as\ntighter have been fairly tested yet. He got\nno punishment to speak of, and until a man is\n\nricked it is hard to tell what staff there is...",
