    text: "DWYER GETS HIS DUST.\nWhat the Champion Has to Say of His\nFuture Intentions.\nSpecial Dispaigh to the Globe-Democrat,\nNrw YorkK, May 17.—Thomas P. Walsh, the\nfinal stakeholder inthe championship prize-\nfight between John Dwyer, of Brooklyn, and\nJim Elliott, of New York, this afternoon noti-\nfied Dwyer to be present at the office of the\nNew York Clipper, and receive the stake\nmoney of $2,000 which he had won by\n‘his victory over Elliott at Long Point, Canada.\nAll the parties assembledin the Clipper office\nat4o’clock. Dwyer was accompanicd by his\nbrother, Alderman William Dwyer, and John\nRoach, an old Fourth Ward sporting man. E/i-'\nliott was not present, nor was he represented\nby friends. |\nMr. Walsh counted out $2,000 in the presence\nof Dwyer, and, handing him the money, said:\n**‘IT surrender to you the stake money in the\nchampionship fight botween yourself and Jim\nElliott, and I hope you may be long able to de-\nfend your title.’’\nDwyer answered, saying: *‘Thank fdu, Mr.\n| Walsh ;, I have done the best I could, and no\nmore.’ ,\n| Daring an interview with a Taporter Dwyer\nsaid: ‘‘I did not want to fight Elliott... I donot\nwant to fight any man. My brother is ar\nAlderman ot Brooklyn, and you know how\nrespectable people regard prize fighting. .\nIaminthe hands of my brother, and I ould\nnot do anything to injure his pipepece in lite;\nbut, if Tom Allen is brought: here, and the-\npeople who are opposed to me use him) for\nthe purpose of taunting me and:\nforcing me from my _ retirement;...",
