    text: "g, Feb , 1882,\n\niG, February o, 1862,\n| ABOUT BARNEYS.\n\nThe Inside History of Some Noted Fistic\n\n_. Events in Which St. Louisans\n\n4 Were Interested.\nBut Little Level Fighting Seen for Teun Years Past\n—How Allen Won and Lost According to the\n| Temper of the Mob—Other Speriing\ni Matters—A Wrestling Mateh\nin Earnest.\n\nWhenever a prize fight is tulked of nowadays\nthe first questions asked are: Do you think it\nwill come off? Wiil they fight on the fevel?\nAnother barn¢y, eh? The rensen of all this is\nthat there have been more barneys than level\nfights during the last ten years, and St, Louis\nhas been specially favored in this connection.\nThere was a duy when the exponents of the\nmanly art were y Ait up to, and when a\ncountry went with its representative. i\n\nWhen Heenan crossed the water to face the\nEhglish champion, /Tom Sayers, the heart of\nof every American was with the Benecia Boy\nand all Englund was with Spryers. To-day\nrepresentatives froin the two conntries might\nMieet without the question of their\nnativity being brought forward at\nall. Now it matters not whether a. man\nbe Breton or Celt, Ajncrican or Canadian ,sothat\nhe can bandle bis |‘‘muauleys’’ better thin lis\nopponent and win the money at stuke. The\nmoney ts whatimen tizht for nowudays, not the\nBory. And right bere is the exuse of the\n\nrieys that have disgraced the ring. —\n\nPerhaps the first) baemey of all was the mect-\ning between Sxvers and Heenan. The luter\n\nve the plucky Eaylishman a terrible beating,\njut Sayers...",
