    text: "ABOUT PRIZE-FIGHTERS.\nSome of the Famous Set-Tos in the His-\ntory of the American Ring.\n\n: Be OS Si\n\nAmong the famous battles fought in the ring\nin this country wus that between James or\n**Yankee’’ Suilivan and ‘‘Vince’’ Hammond.\nIt was fought in the early part of the year\n1841 on League island, near Philadelphia, and\nHammond suffered a bad defeat. One week\nafter Tom Hyer fought and whipped ‘*Coun-\ntryvy’’ McClusky up on the Hudson, the fight\ngrowes out of the quarrel over Sullivan’s al-\n\n— ability ‘‘to whip any one, bar none.’’\n\nn May, 1847, Sullivan beat Robert Count,\nthe fight being over even rounds, occupy-\ning twelve minutes. ext came Hyer and Sul-\nlivan in a fight for $5,000 a side, at Rock Point,\nMd.,in whicn Hyer came off victorious after\nsixteen rounds. Hyer then claimed the cham.\npionship of America.\n\nFour years after, John Morrissey appeared as\na candidate for  dygs sae honors, and on Octo-\nber 12, 1853, he fought Sullivan at Boston\nCorners for $1,000 a side, whipping the latter\nafter thirty-seven rounds, Gocupying fitty-five\nminutes.\n\nSullivan died in San Francisco May 31, 1856,\naged 45 years.\n\nOn October 20, 1858, John ©. Heenan\n(‘‘Benicia Boy’’) and Morrissey fought at Long\nPoint, Canada, for $2,500 a side, Morrissey\nagain winning in twenty-five minntes, after\neleven rounds. This ended Morris<ey’s career\nas a prize-fighter, though on several occasions\nhe appeared in public with the gloves for chari-\ntable purposes. _\n\nIn May, 1863, Mike McCoole and Joe...",
