    text: "The Ring.\nTOM ALLEN’S CHALLENGE.\nTo the Editor of the Herald:\n\nHaving returned to this country after six\nyears’ sojourn in England, where I had a\nstanding challenge to fight any manin Great\nBritain, I find that during my absence tliere\nbavé been three battles for the heavy-weight\nehampionship, which title I held until my bat-\ntle with Joe Goss in 1876. Now, Johnny Dwyer\nis dead, Paddy Ryan-bas retired, and Joe Goss\nis on the shelf, but there are James Elliott,\nGeorge Rooke and last; but not least, John L.\nSullivan, who holds the title of champion.\n\nI will cy gr John L. Sullivan or Any man in\nthe world, according to the rules of the Lon-\ndon prize ring, at catch weight, for $1,600. or\n§5,000 a side and the ehampionship of the\nworld. My backer has posted $1,000 to back up\nthis challenge, and I will be ready to meet\nSullivan er any pugilist at the Police Gazctte\noffice, to arrange a match, on Novesnper i6,\nthe fight to take place in three months within\n100 miles of New Orleans. Harry Hill to be\nfinal stukeholder. - ’ TOM ALLEN.\n\nNew York, November 2, 1832. =\n\nf¥rom the Boston Hefald.| -\n\nThere is considerable excitement manifested\nin sporting circles over a proposed prize fight,\nfor $2,500 2 side, between Tom Alien, ex-chain-\nage a of America,and Jobin L. Sullivan,\nof thiscity. Richard K+ Fox's Le pirnardenge velo\narrived here yesterday, with tull power.from\nFox to match Allen against the Boston cham-\n\njong Sullivan’s backer says he will name\nKo vember 10 to meet and sign...",
