    text: "> 3 ee é -\n— -2 = ves. =\nTom Allen Arrested as a Fugitive from\nJustice—The New Orieans Meeeting\nSporting Miscellany.\n: ———_—\n\nConsiderable excitement was caused among\na doen element of the sporting fratern-\nity ay atternoon by the arrest of Toi\nAllen, the ex-champion of America, at his\nsaloon, on Market street, between Sixth and\nSeventh streets, upon the charge of being a fu-\ngitive from Kentucky justice. The arrest was\nmade quietly by Officer Thomas Donegan, 0!\nthe Central District Office, at the in-\nstance of Chief Harrigan, at abou!\n3080 o’cloek. Allen remained in the hold-ove!\nuntil about 8 o’clock, when be was taken up-\nstairs into the office of the Chief’s Secretary\nwhere, about 9:30, he was enabled to give 4\nbond of $2,000, with Joseph M. Schnaider,\nbrewer, and Frederick Watkins, bide and\nleather merchant, as securities for his ap-\npearance before Judge Noonan w-morrow.\nJudge Chester H. Krum, Thomas Morris\nand Frank Turner represented Allen, Judge\nNoonan and Col. Claiborne being present at\nthe signing of the bond.\n\nThe trouble originated with the fight e-\ntween Allen and Joe Goss in September, 1876,\nnear Covington, Ky. Both were arrested at\nthe time, Goss standing trial] «nd being fined\n$300, which he paid. Allen gave bond of $3,000,\nwith Patrick Murpby,. of Cincinnsti, and W.\nJ. Farrell and bis brother, of Covington, as\nsureties, aher which he leit for Enzland, re-\nmaining away for five years. He returned to\nSt. ‘Louts abouta year und a hulf wgo, und\nwbont a. year...",
