    id: "705",
    text: "General Sporting Notes.\n\n—Mr. Chas. KE. Mussey, for many years a\nroom-kKeeper in this city, is here making ar-\nrangements for removing his family to Chi-\nsago, where he is now engaged in business.\nHis and his family’s friends dislike to see\nthem leave.\n\n—A few days ago the REPUBLICAN had an\nitem about a candy-eating dog, but Capt. Bill\nRichards, who has charge of the Union base\nball grounds, offers to match his Scoeteh Skye\nrerrier, Lillie, against any other dog in this\nsection ot the country for drinking lager\nbeer.\n\n—The first championship game played at\nUnion base ball grounds took place yester-\nJay, and Capt. Wm. Richards secured the\npail, which he intends having gilded, and\nthen, under instructions from Mr. Lucas,\nhe will present it to Tom Allen, who will\nhave it Swuug up in his saloon on Market\nstreet.\n\n—Thomas ht. Bullock of Philadelphia pro-\nposes to publish a billiard manual andhand-\nbook of reference, yiving the names of all\nprominent room-keepers throughout the\nsountry, their location, the number of tables\nused, largest runs ever made in each room\nand by whom made. This will bea great con-\nvenlence to all room-kKeepers. Manager Don-\novan of the Monarch has ordered eight\ncopies.\n\n—Nothing of special interest occurred in bill-\nlard circles here during the past week. Busi-\nness was very fair bothatthe Monarch and\nat Mussey’s, but no match games were\nplayed. Secarcely a particle of interest is\nmunifested in the Schaefer-Slosson contest,\nalthough «li seem to tink it...",
