    id: "725",
    text: "GONE TO THE DOGS.\nNoted Prize-Fighters Who Took to\n\nDrink Because of Defeat in the Ring.\n\n{From the Minneapolis Tribune. ]\n\n**T see,’’ said a well-Enown Minneapolis\nthumper yesterday evening to a reporter for\nthe Tribune, ‘‘that the young woman who\nwas knocked out by the dark girl dressed in\nDlue at Market Hall the other night, has taken\nto drink in consequence of her defeat. It may\nlook to peaple who are not in this sort of busi-\nness that such people are fools, but I tell you\nIhave seen many a big man go to pieces\nunder the disappointment of defeat. Let me\ntell you about a few of them:\n\n**Do you remember the once celebrated\nMike McCoole? I knew Mike when he had more\ntriends than any man in St. Louis. He Kkepta\n— on Fifth street, which was nightly\n\ned with admirers, and it used to be con-\nsidered an honor to shake hands with him.\nIremember him when he was in the height of\nhis glory, and there were leading people who\ndidn’t hesitate to visif his place and solicit\nhis acquaintance. He was a great, big,\nignorant, steamboat roustabout, but in a\nfight on Bloody Island he knocked his op-\nponent’s phiz out of en and this gave him |\n& reputation such as John L. Sullivan enjoys\nto-day. But Tom Allien, an English bruiser,\ncame along, and Tom was never satisfied\nuntil he had a meeting with Mike. McCooile\nmarried the daughter of well-to-do Irish\narents, and at the wedding there were\nFively times. The presents were handsome\nand the costumes elegant. Mike wore a\nswallow-tailed coat and...",
