    localCopyFull: "images/articles/image-731b.jpg",
    id: "731",
    text: "JACK AND JOE.\neS ee a a\nTHE CHANCES OF A FIGHT BETWEEN DEMP-\nSEY AND ILLINGSWORTH.\n\nNew York Clubmen Anxious to See Dlings-\nworth “‘Do” Dempsey—The Ring Record of\nthe Famous Irish Pugilist Dan Donnelly—\n*‘Deaf’’ Burke at New Orileans—Sullivan vs.\nSmith—Macon’s Melange.\n\nSpecial Correspondence ofthe POst-DISPATCH.\nPHILADELPHIA, April 1.—Sporting men here-\n\nabouts do not view with favor the report that\n\ncomes from England of the willingness of the\nfriends of Jem Smith to back him against Sul-\nlivan for $5,000, especially as they see by read-\ning between the lines that an effort is being\nmade to ‘‘gammon” the “big fellow” into fight-\ningin England. The English are famed for\ntheir skill in making matches. They have a\nproverb which says ‘‘a match well made is\nhalf won,’’ and itis evident that in yielding\nto Sullivan the amount of the stakes,\nthey intend to exact from him a con-\ncession as to the battle place, which will\ngive himlittle or no chance to win them.\n\nThis is plain from the fact that they say that\n\nSmith would haye no chanoe to win in Amer-\n\nica, astatement which has no foundation in\n\nfact. English fighters, asarule, have been\n\nexceedingly well treated inthis country. I\n\nknow of but one exception, that of ‘‘Deaf’’\n\nBurke, at New Orleans, where he was chased\n\nout of the ring when he was besting one\n\nO’Connell. That was over forty years ago.\n\nSince then Ben Caunt, Bob Caunt, Tom Allen,\n\nJoe Goss, George Ryall, Ned O’Baldwin, Jem\n\nMace, Charley Mitchell, Jack Burke, -...",
