    text: "CAPT. DALTON.\nThe Chicago Pugilist Who Is to Meet Jack\nKing—His Record.\n\nCapt. Dalton, the Chicago pugilist, who is to\nmeet Jack King at the People’s Theater on\nSaturday nightina six-round glove contest,\ncalied at the PoST-DISPATCH office this morn-\ning-\n\n‘*Itis not generally kKnown,’’ said the Cap-\ntain, ‘‘that I am a blood relative of Charley\nC. Gallagher, who fought Tom Alien here a\nfew years ago. My mother and Gallagher\nwere sister and brother, so that you see [come\nfrom a fighting family.’’\n\n**How much do you weigh, Captain?’’\n\n- €*One hundred and bee: gem pounds.\n\nYou can see I have.not a pound of superfiuous\n\nfiesh about me, for before training for this\nmill I had arranged to meet Mitchell and was\ngetting in trim for my match with him when\n\narrangements for the present mill were con-\nsummated.’’\n\n**Whom have you fought?’’\n\n*‘Imet Paddy Ryan at McCormick’s Hall,\n\n‘Chicago, and fought him a draw. It wasa\nfour-round small-giove tight. After Ryan, I\nstood before Sullivan four rounds. I have\nalse met Burke, Jimmy Elliott, Mike Cleary,\nJohn P. Clair, Johnand Mike Driscoll, Jim\nGoode and many others.’’-\n\n, ‘‘Whom do you consider the best of the\nlot?’\n\n_ ‘Why Sullivan, of course, and then after\n| him I look upon Jim Goode as good as any of\nthem. My hardest fight was with him. I\nstopped himin three rounds.’’\n\nTom —- was getting his boxers together -\nto-day ior Saturday night’s preliminary bouts.\n‘*You can bet,’’he said, ‘*that Saturday night’s\nexhibition will furnish the St....",
