    text: "eee ean\nee\nmentioned as ore willing to accept the\nhag ree: engi ed it was put in pps\norm, but nothing has since been hear from\nthe broker. Why Is it these two men, both\nfine exponents of the manly art, cannot be\npeougs* together? A match of six or eight\nrounds, the winner to take 75 and the ioser 25\nper cent. of the receipts. Such a match\nwould fill any hall in the city and would\nstimulate boxing more than anything that\ncoulc be gotten up.\n\nThe recent benefit tendered Charles Daly\nwas a fair indication of the class of people\nwho patronize athletes of all kinds when\nproperly conducted. The gathering embraced\nall the most prominent men ot\nthe city, in every walk of life, and it\nwas an orderly and well-behaved audience.\n\nThere 1s no reason why these entertainments\nshould not take place once or twice a month.\nThey would be of great advantage to the boys.\nDid Father Tom Kelly has been working tor\nyears to elevate the sport, and has done won-\njers for its advancement. The late benefit\ngiven by the Daly boys Ws the key-note to\nthe great success boxing will be in St. Louts\nduring the future. With the ex-champion\nheavy-weight of the world in our midst, the\nex-champion middie-welght only a few doors\nfrom him, with Tom Allen and Tom Kelly to\nteach the youngsters the ropes, and with such\ntalent as we possess for learning, and when\nonce learned put into practice, St.Louls should\nstand in the front rank as the premier resort\nof the champions in boxing as in all other\nclasses of sport.",
