    text: "SS ee\nINTERNATIONAL PUGILISM,\nBig Men of the Past Who Crossed the Water\nand Fought Well.\nT. P. Evans in Boston Globe.\n\nNever in the nistory of the prize-ring has\nGreat Britain been invaded with such an array\nof foreign pugilists as at present. From the\ntime when pugilism first obtained a foothold\nin the United States, champions not content\nwith victories on their own soil, went abroad\nto do battle with the sons of Britain. The\ngreatest of these upheld the dignity of the con-\ntinent with unparalleled success.\n\nMollineux, the colored champion, did battle\nwith the redoubtable Tom Cribb, and the\nhistory of the prize ring tells us that had the\nwarrior been shown fair play the undefeated\nCribb would have been worsted. Charley\nFreeman, on British ground, upheld his own\nuntil he met with an at 2 death.\n\nYankee Sullivan braved the perils of the At-\nlantic to meet the gailant Hammer Lane, and\nhe succeeded in placing the stars and stripes\nabove the union jack. All judges of pugilism\nwho witnessed the contest pronounced him\nthe greatest phenomenon that ever put\nup a hand. Charley Lynch met the\nbest men of his weight, and encountered\nsuch shining lights of the prize-ring as Simon\nFinighty and Young Shaw. Bob Smith, the\ncolored wonder, rurely ever talked about, was,\nin the writer’s opinion, one of the greatest\nfighters thet ever lived. His decisive victories\nover Tom and Harry Allen remain as two of\nthe hardest figets ever recorded in the history\nof the prize-ring.\n\nOt the number who have...",
