    localCopyFull: "images/articles/image-753b.jpg",
    id: "753",
    text: "M’MANUS-FLINT MILL.\nSE\nTOM ALLEN GIVES HIS OPINION OF ‘THE\nPRIZE FIGHT YESTERDAY.\n\nFlint Failed to Take His Second’s Advice and\nLost the Battle Thereby—Incidents of the\nTrip—Kilrain and Mitchell Versus the\nAmerican Exchange—Base Ball News and\nGossip—Sporting Matters. ° ;\n\nThe result of the prize fight between Mo-\nManus and Flint near St. Clair, Mo., yester-\nday, was disoussed pro and con to-day among\nthe sports about town. The general opinion\nseemed to be that the St. Louis man had the\nbest of the battle and would undoubtedly have\nwon it had he not given up at the merest show\nof rally on the part of McManus.\n\nTOM ALLEN, )\nwho seconded Flintin the fight, sald to-day\nthat his man inflicted more punishment on |\nMcManus than the latter served to Flint and\nthat, although not beaten much about tbe\nhead and face beyond aswollen cheek and a\ncut lip, he sustained severe punishment about\nthe body. ‘‘If you noticed it,’’ said Allen,\n*‘all the fighting was done over in the other\nman’s corner. Four biows were not struck\noutside that corner, and if Flint had taken)\nmy advice and waited for his man\nto face him as he should have done the result\nwould be different. I kepttelling him to stay\nin his corner, not to go out over to the other\nman’s and that he’d win but, no, he knew\nmore about it thanI did and he got licked.\nFlint is a more acientific fighter than McManus\nand he should have walked on that line, If\nscience is your hold you ought to make science\nyour play. Itold himto go at his man,...",
    summary: "St. Louis Post-Dispatch reported Tom Allen's commentary on a recent prize fight, noting that Flint failed to follow his second's advice and lost the battle.",
    focus: "secondary",
    tier: "2",
    not_tom: false
