    text: "TOM ALLEN,\nRetired Champion Pugilist of the World, 619\nMarket street. Free concert; also, boxing by\nthe most scientific boxers of the city every\nevening.\nOO\nTHE ORIGIN OF INN SIGNS,\n\nQUEER OLD HOSTELRIES THAT NESTLE IN\n\nTHE ROMANTIC MOUNTAINS ACCOUNT FOR\n\nIT,\n\nWhat is the origin of signs? The earliest\nsigns are ceftainly heraldic. We have still in\nmany Willages the ‘‘So-and-so Arms,’’ with\nthe shi¢id of the lord of the manor em-\nbiazoned upon it, with all its quarterings. |\nor we have the Red Lion, or\nthe White Hart, or the Swan, all either crests\nor cognizances of a family, or of a sovereign |\nor queen. The Swan sign is said to date from\nAnne of Cleves; the White Hart was the badge |\nof Richard Il., and inns with this sign proba-\nbly were erected in that reign, and have kept.\nthis sign unchanged since. We know of inns\nunder the name of the Rose, whichthere can\nbe little question came into life as hostelries |\nin the time of the Yorkists and Lancastrians. |\nThe Wheat Sheaf was the Burleigh badge, the\nElephant that of Beaumont, the Bull’s\nHead was a Boleyn cognizance, the\nBlue Boar the badge of the De\nVeres, earls of Oxford, the Green Dragon\nof the earls of Pembroke, the Falcon of the |\nMarquis of Winchester. It does not, however, |\nfollow that the inns that have these signs date\nfrom the periods when, let us say, Anne\nBoleyn was queen, because they bear the\ntoken of the bull’s head, or from the reign of\nQueen Elizabeth, when Burleigh was in\npower, because of the wheat sheaf,...",
