    text: "bie of as bountiful Mogg d Bg acd land.\naisoof the finest qual fm such great\nTomatees-are selling Boe qjbasbe! for tne\n\n: e\nand b frutts and than any\n\n4 Wholesale Mittiner, To an outsider the\nwholesale millinery trade can never be even\nfaintly imagined. The dimculties and tnou-\nsand details aré Wonderful. To see the de-\npartment where the feathers alone are\nstocked one would !magine that the forests\nof America and Europe both were destitute\nof one ieathered songster at least. You can\nnot imagine how s0 many could have been\nkilled and yet have any remaining. Our\ncustom, of course, is to send large orders,\nespecially to the country, and only in a\nwholesale way, and itis amusing to see the\ndifference tn our tg ae The men come\nin and go business-like to work to make\nKnown their credit, and elicit inquiries from\nus. With most of the women, however, they\nwish to ignore all such proceedings, and then\nwe a make inquiries. They oftem,an-\nswer by floods of tears or indignant qfes.\ntions asto whether ‘‘they have. not Zood\nfaces,’’ and yet they want, on the strength\nof their good faces alone, to order an im-\nmense line of goods. We generally manage\nthem que but firmly, and, to their credit\nbe it said, they nearly alwa¥s are very con-\nscientious In paying. We seldom, it ever,\nlose a cent through a woman customer.\n\nLew Harper, Lindell Hotel. Between bell-\nboys, reporters and guests we hotel clerks\nare in tough luck. There’s Mr. Isaac Levy,\nof New York, who's just left this desk. Lur-\ning...",
