    text: "VERY FEW OF THEM WEALTHY,\niow the Champion Pugilists Made\nMoney and Lost it.\n\nFrom the New York Herald, .\n\nMore reckless with his suddenly a\nwealth than the gambler is the successful pu-\ngilist, The profession, especially at present,\nis a lucrative one, but the fighters who have\nretained fortunes after making them can\nreadily be counted on the fingers, while the\nspendthrift gladiators are numberless. Sud-.\nden relaxation from severe training periods\nmay account for much of this profligacy, Cer-\ntain it is that no class of men are more thor-\noughly satisfied’ to let the morrow look out for\nitself than champion pugiliSts.\n\nNo better example of an extravagant\n\npugilist exists than John L. Sullivan, It\nwas he who developed the knock-out blow,\nand by making 4, 6 and 10 round contests as\n\nfull of uncertainty and danger as tho old\n\n‘fitish fights mado pugilism the remunera-\n\n| tive profession that it now is. ‘hough\n\ngrossly overestimated the “Big Fellow’s”’\n\n\"earnings have been treble those of any fighter\nwho came before him, yet Sullivan now has\nlittle or no money. Jake Kilrain is said to be\n\nas extravagant as was John L,\n\n- Joe Coburn, whoin his old age has had to\nhustle for dollars in match making\nwith other old _ timers, is another\nwarning example. Jem Mace, whose\nbenefit in Londen the other day was noted,\nis another. Mace and Coburn were the won-\nders of their day, and though they didn’t\nmake as much money in fights or exhibitions\nas the latter}day champions they made more\n\nin...",
    summary: "The St. Louis Republic reported that jem Ward,one of England's greatest heavyweights, died not long ago at the age of 86, in the semi-charitable Licensed Victuallers' Institution. Tom Sayers, another famed champion of England, would have died in want but for a life pension presented him after his memorable drawn battle with John C.",
