    id: "789",
    text: "BOOK-BETTING on the Eastern races to-\nGay and every evening during the races\nat the Fair Grounds by\n\nJ. G@. HAMBRICE & Co.\nSporting Notes.\n\nTom ALLEN, retired champion pugilist of\nthe world, 619 Marketstreet. Free concert\nevery evening. The best cf singers only eo-\ngaged.\n\nA NARROW ESCAPE.\nA Heavy Stone Falls from a Third\n‘Story to the Sidewalk.\n\nMr. G. H. Lippelt, manager of the Zitt-\nlosen Tent and Awniiag Company, at No.\n307 North Fourth street, had a narrow 685-\ncape from instant death atabout 9 o’clock\nyesterday morning. He had just started to\nleave his.place of business when a stone\nwindow cap weighing about 250 pounds\nfellfrom the third-story above his head.\nMr. Lippelt heard a woman scream, and he\njumped back justin time to escape being\nStruck by the stone which fell just in ad-\nVance of where he was. The stone\nmade a hole about. 4 feet in diame-\nter through the six-inch stone sidewalk\nand frightened Mr. Lippeltso that he was\nfaint for some timeafterward. It isallegea\nthat the accident was caused by the-under-\nmining of the north wall of the building by\nthe excavations made for the new seéeven-\nstory Catlin building. The wall has been\nbraced, but it is said that It Is nevertheless\nsettling, and that, asa consequence, the\n‘lils and caps of the building have been\nloosened and cracks have appeared In vari-\nous parts of the buliding. Some of the em-\npioyes have dectined to work longer In tne\nbuilding on account of their apprehension\nof danger... Commissioner Furlong...",
