    text: "OBURN'S CAREER.\n\nfe Whipped Many Good Menand Es-\n\ncaped Defeat.\n\nJoe Coburn is dead. Twenty-five years |\nio he was the Sullivan of the day and. was_\n-onsidered the master of Mace and the qual\n1f Heenan or Morrissey. Not only was he\n. great ring fighter, but he had the reputation\nif being the best bar-room fighterin America,\nhough Sherman Taurston, of this city, could\n‘ive him a hard go at this particular style of\nvarfare.\n\nCoburn was born in County Armagh, Ire-\nand, on July 4, 1835, and came to this country\nvhen 5 years of age. He grew up in New\nYork City, and at the age of 18 he became\nvrominent as a fighter by whipping al! the\n,oys in the Sixteenth Ward.\n\nCobura first became known to fame as a\nicater by defeating “Awful” Gardner,\nne of the cleyerest men of his day,\n\n‘ie six rounds. He was then\nsoked upon as a coming champion. About\nhis time Ned Price, a noted English boxer,\n\n‘ut now a well-known criminal lawyer,\n\nhallenged any man in the world to dehi\n\nim. Coburn went to Boston and arranged a\n\nsateh, They met at Still Pond, > pag\n\nnd after three hours and twenty-five min-\ntes of desperate fighting the referee de-\nlared the battle a draw oa account of\narkness. Both men *were contined to\nheir rooms fortwo wecks from tbe terrible\n\nsunishment they received. Coburn issued a\n\nhallenge to fight*Price again, but the latter\n\nefused. Tbis was Coburn’s first London\nule contest and t®s battle was onc of the\neireatest ever witnessed in America up to |\n\npat period. i\n\nAfter deteating...",
