    localCopyFull: "images/articles/image-810b.jpg",
    id: "810",
    text: "THE RING.\n\nMany champions have falien hard and suad-\nden, but few with the severity and speed of\nJack Dempsey, writes Macon. His skill and\nquickness were so great that, though he was\nnever more thana good ten-stone man, his.\nMany victims were men bigger, stronger and\nheavier than himself, and finally deceived\nhim and his admirers into the belief that,bar-\nring the champion of allchampions, he was_\nvirtually invincible. |\n\nHis defeat by La Blanche did not naturally\nchange this opinion, forit was looked upon\nasanunilucky fluke due to a chance blow,\nput the pitiable exhibition he made with Bob\nFitzsimmons emphasizes in the most marked\nmanner the old sporting proverb: ‘‘A good\nlittle man cannot whip a good big one.’’\n\nNow let me say right here that, though\nthere was less than five pounds difference in\nthe weight of the Australian and Dempsey,\nin reality Jack was at least two sizes smaller\nthan his opponent. This difference was so\napparent when they faced each other for the\nfirst round that a gentleman who sat inthe\nbox immediately behind my seat exclaimed:\n“*Why,this man Fitzsimmons is big enough\nto fight Sullivan!’’ and so he was. Dempsey\nlooked like a pigmy in front of him.\n\nThe Australianisthe biggest man for his\nweight leversaw, with one exception, and\nthat was the late Tom Ryan, the sporting |\nprinter. Tom was five feet ten inches tall, if\nIremember rightly, and could fight well and |\nstrong at 108 pounds. Ihave seen him spar\nwith Joe Coburn, Tom Allienand Jem Mace,\nand...",
