    localCopyFull: "images/articles/image-889b.jpg",
    id: "889",
    text: "**Prize fights aren’t what they used to be,’’\nsaid Andrew Carlson, of New Orleans, at the\nSouthern. **The replacing of the London\nrules with the Queensberry and the introduc-\ntion of boxing gloves into championship bat-\ntlles have relieved such contests. of much o!\ntheir whilom brutality. It is very seldom\nnow that you see a blood-curdling encounter\nin the equates circle. Science has given it\npometTefinement, and it is possible to say,\nwith much show of truth of some ring en-\ncounters, that they are ‘nice, quiet, genteel,\ngentlemanly, affairs,’ as I heard the referee\nsay when Dan Daly was sprinkling everybody\nat the ring-side with young Ed Kelly’s blood\njust outside of this city some yearsago. I\nsaw Tom Allen and Mike McCoole fight down\nthe river in the good old days when steam-\nboating was still afive, and I saw long-legged\nCharley Gallaghefdoup Tom Allen in three\nterrific rounds somewhere near Chester, Il.\nThere was fighting at that time. Men were\nchopped to pieces in the ringand fairly rained\ngore. The prise fight of the present day isn’!\nAny more like those ‘gos’ than Mrs. Highflyer’s\n§ Oo*clock teais like a railroad collision ora\nkeg of tenpenny nails doing an all-sasshay in\nB dynamite explosion.’’",
