    text: "THE FORTUNES IN FISTS.\ne ———\nOld-Time Prize-Fighters Contended for\nVery Modest Stakes.  _\n\nPrize-fighting has always been a profitabie\nbusiness. The men of brawn who have en-\ntered and ornamented it have made moreout\nof this use of their strength and muscies than\nthey could have done by employing them in\npeaceful channels. The rewards now are\nlarger and more alluring than they ever were\nLook at James J. Corbett—a few years a bank\nclerk, one year ago a Common wage-earner in\nthe -pugilistic ranks, to whom $5000 was\n\n‘bigger than Pike’s Peak; to-day he owas a\nVilla by the sea, can sign his cueck for $30,-\n| 0U0 or $40,000, and is making $10U0 to $2000\na week by giving feeble imitations of bad act-\ny ing. He Fes his _Croesusian start when he\nnocked Jobn L. Sullivan out in the twenty-\nfirstround at New Orleans last September\nand won the stake money, $20,00.,anda\npurse of $25,000. This is the largest amount\never fought for by eg gilists. litzsimmons\nand Hall fought for the same amount, $45,-\n’ 600, last March, and it was all purse money,\nthere being no stake. Charley Mitchell came\never hereand challenged Corbett, expecting\n| that some athletic club would put upa purse\nof $75,000 to $100,000, but the iargest offer\nto date fand it is ‘likely to be the only offer) is\na purse of $40,090 pramised by the Coney\nIsland Athletic Club. Thisis a smal} fortune,\nwhich may be earned with a single jab on the\npoint of the other man’s chin.\n\nGlove fights have been the zouue now for\nabout twelve...",
