    text: "Even the excitement of the Civil War\nwas not sufficient to obliterate interest in\nfistic contests, and when ‘the late unpleas-\nantness’’ was over it found Joe Coburn,\nchampion of America, and a pretty able\nlist of lesser fistic lights revolving around\nhim. Dominick Bradley and Sam Rankin,\nwho had fought a prize battle for ‘the\nhonor and glofy of God,\"’ had both retired,\nbut big Mike McCool was very much in evi-\ndence, and he and Ton? Allen made things\npugilistic very lively in the West. ‘‘The\nIrish Gtant,’’ Ned O' Baldwin, had been fol-\nlowed to this country by Jem Mace, and\nthey, with Jose Donnald, were keeping up\ntheir end in this part of the country.\nO'’Baldwin was jailed, and Mace, after\nfights with Coburn and Allen, left the land.\nThen there was quite a fistic lull, broken by\nspasmodic intervals of activity, such as\nwhen Johnny Dwyer beat Jimmy Elliott.\nAt one time two bald-headed old men, Joe\nGoss and Tom Allen, fought for the cham-\npionship of America. Even I thought the\ngame was pretty near moribund then.",
