    text: "ae neal\nOne of the Old School of Hard\nFighters.\n\nIn the discussion as to the best man to\nreferee the coming Corbett-Fitzsimmons\nfight many names have been suggested, but\nuntil recently Western men have evinced\nlittle interest as to whom they prefer to\nsee in this responsible position. George\nSiler of Chicago was suggested, but Corbett\npromptly rejected him, owing to vigorous\nviews expressed by Siler against the cham-\n| pion,\n\nBob Farrell, the veteran boxing instruct-\nor and trainer of St. Louis, has been sug-\ngested by the Denver sporting contingent\nand if he should be selected it is a foregone\nconclusion that the men will have to fight,\nand on the square, until one of them is\nwhipped, for Farrell comes from the old\nschool of fighters—the class that would go\nout before breakfast and fight to a finish\nwith bare knuckles for a purse of $1@.\nWhen Farrell was in his prime men fought\nwith their hands and did not fill up the\ncolumns of the papers with a lot of rot\n‘about what they were going todo. They did\ntheir deeds first and told about them after-\nward.\n\n- Besides having been one of the cleverest\nlightweights in his day, Farrell his had\na great deal of ring experience. He as-\nsisted in training John L. Sullivan for his\nfight with Paddy Ryan in 1882, and seconded\nJack Dempsey in his fight with Turnbull\nin New York in 1882. Bob seconded George\nFulljames, who defeated Gallagher and\nafterward gave Dempsey such a hard bat-\ntle. He trained Coburn for his last fight\nwith Mace, and...",
