    text: "PUGILISTIC CAREER\nOF PADDY RYAN,\nFought His Way Into Prominence\nby Thrashing Tough\nCanal Men.\nSULLIVAN WAS HIS UNDOING.\nUniil the Battle at) Mississippi\n| City. Miss., Ryan Was Consis-\nered the Premier in the |\nFighting World.\nEY MACON MeCORMICK.\nPSLRA CORRESPONDENCE\n\nNew York, Dee. 15.—Poor old Paddy Ryan\nis dead to the world at last. Pugilistically\nhe has been “a dead one’ for many years.\nNe was in his forty-eighth year and should\nhave been in the meridian of manhood\n\nPaddy came to this country when but a\nslip of a boy, and was raised in West Troy\nWithin a mile of the spot where John ©.\nHleenan, “The Benicia Koy,” first sow the\nlight of day, and just across the Hudson\nRiver from where John Morrissey passed\nhis early boyhood. Paddy grew up to be a\nline specimen of physical manhood, standing\nsix feet in his stockings und weighing in\nthe neighborhood of 200 pounds. He passed\n& good deal of his boyhood in the vicinity\nof the “Birst Lock” in West Troy, and had\nmany an eneounter there with the tough\ncitizens who navigated the waters of the\nerie Canal ‘The Canallers” were, as a\nrato, brawny fellows, aud among them the\nrile “might makes right” prevailed very\nextensively, Before he was 2l Paddy lke\nthe here of the old seng\n\n“Could handle any man on the Canawl.”\n\nThere are many “tips” in and about Troy\nand Ryan secon beeame their fistic idol.\n\nShortly after Johnny Dwyer of Brooklyn\nbecame champion of America by defeating\nJimmy Elfiett (Yourg Grayback) he cnade\n# sparring tour...",
